_No response_

### Additional Information

In order to get around this, one has to do this, which seems fragile.
I have not tested if the `winX === point.x && winY === point.y + 1` is robust with all window display modes (fullscreen, frameless, framed, different windows themes, etc).

```typescript
// track all modifiers changes here, because we don't know what they will be during the 'system-context-menu' event.
let modifiers: KeyboardInputEvent["modifiers"] = [];
primaryWindow.webContents.on("before-input-event", (event, input) => {
    if (input.type === "keyDown" || input.type === "keyUp") {
        modifiers = input.modifiers as KeyboardInputEvent["modifiers"];
    }
});

primaryWindow.on("system-context-menu", (evt, point) => {
    const [winX, winY] = primaryWindow.getPosition();
    if (winX === point.x && winY === point.y + 1) {
        // not sure why point.y is one less than winY, but we'll go with it.
        // sure enough of a bet that this was triggered by alt+space rather than a right click on the 'app-region: drag;'
        evt.preventDefault();
        primaryWindow.webContents.sendInputEvent({
            type: "keyDown",
            keyCode: "Space",
            modifiers: modifiers,
        });
    }
});
```

There is no way to empirically know if the system-context-menu event was triggered by a right-click on the drag app-region or if it was triggered by ALT+SPACE except to check `winX === point.x && winY === point.y + 1` - which I maintain is fragile. That is less of an issue than the fact that I have to manually trigger the space keydown, even though the system-context-menu is prevented.

